into sport utility vehicles  what's the story behind the secret yale  organization skull and bones whose  membership includes both the current and  former presidents bush  in secrets of the tomb  a yale graduate and secret society  member takes us into our alma mater to  find out this talk from the yale  university bookstore in new haven  connecticut is about a half an hour  thanks um thanks very much for coming  can you hear me in the back  can you hear me now  all right this is not gonna be easy  okay um i have to say this is sort of a  weird situation for me um at my other  book reading sometimes after the event  was over a member of a secret society  it's happened with manuscript now and  it's happened with brasilias has come up  to me and talked about the book and  admitted that they were members but this  is the first time i've done a reading  where there are probably members of  skull and bones in the audience  among you right now and there are  probably members of my own secret  society among you here as well in fact  i think i recognize a few although i'm  not going to point them out for you so i  should say at the outset that  if you feel that during my talk or  during the question and answer period  i'm not being quite as forthcoming as i  might otherwise be if there weren't  members of my secret society in the  audience  email me at  secretsofthetomb.com where we can have a  private side conversation  a lot of people have asked me why i  decided to try to investigate skull and  bones and the bottom line is really that  skull and bones is an irresistible  opportunity for a reporter looking for a  challenge  here you have a secret society perhaps  the or at least one of the most infamous  secret societies in the world this is a  society with setting its headquarters is  a practically windowless building called  the tomb the tea the temple or the  boodle and it's located here at yale  uh skullenbones also owns a private  island they've owned it since the turn  of the 20th century it's about 350 miles  from new york city on the saint lawrence  river  this is also a story with characters at  any given time there are about 800  living members of skull and bones these  members in these members include people  like george w bush many other bushes  senator john kerry which could make the  presidential election of 2004  particularly interesting if it becomes  the first bones versus bones ballot  pulitzer prize-winning biographer david  mccullough is a member of skull bones as  was avril harriman henry stimson and  even steve greenberg the former deputy  commissioner of major league baseball  all of these members know to keep quiet  about the society's secrets and in fact  in 2000 when george w bush was  campaigning for presidents  the society actually sent a letter  reportedly to all members reminding them  that they had better keep quiet about  the society's secrets especially to  members of the media  this is also a story with mystery the  society is fraught with rumors secrets  and alleged conspiracies skull and bones  is said to operate a secret world  government it's rumored to be a wealthy  landowner that guarantees its members  power and financial security for life  and conspiracy theorists have managed to  pin onto skull and bones everything from  foreign policy disasters to one of my  personal favorites uh the horrors of the  dewey decimal system  [Laughter]  so with setting characters and mystery  i figured that skull and bones would be  a fun interesting and difficult story on  which to report  but of course there was more to it than  that there always is so i thought i'd  tell you a little bit more about my  reasons behind  my decision to try to investigate skull  and bones  first of all when i was at yale i  thought that secret societies were sort  of fading i thought they were on their  way out and that people my age just  didn't care so much anymore  but about a year after i graduated i  heard a revealing story  about a girl who was in my class the  class of 1998.  this was a girl who was one of the  prominent figures on campus  if there was a major activity going on  she had inevitably played some part in  organizing it and so people just assumed  that when her senior year rolled around  when she was missing from  parties dining halls and bars every  thursday and sunday night like the rest  of the secret society members that she  inevitably must have been tapped by  skull and bones or another major secret  society  well  sometime before she graduated probably  about three or four weeks beforehand her  roommate found her on a thursday night  she had not in fact been attending  secret society meetings every thursday  and sunday night instead she was hiding  in a weenie bin in the top floor of  sterling memorial library so that  everybody would think that she had been  tapped for a secret society because she  was so mortified that she had not been  as a prominent figure on campus i  thought that was kind of a telling story  another reason i was intrigued by skull  and bones was how widespread the rumors  are and how readily people tend to  accept them  here's an anecdote from the book dealing  with that this actually happened to me  about a year and a half ago when i  visited yale for an alumni convention  george bush was one of the featured  speakers that weekend so i had decided  that i was going to spend the afternoon  staking out the skull and bones tomb  waiting for george bush to come by and  if he walked by and tried to get into  the tomb i had it in my head that i was  going to confront him um he never came  by but  this sort of interesting scene happened  and this is an excerpt from the book  so here i am sitting in front of the  tomb  two new haven police officers pedal by  in their bicycles then circle back in  front of the skullenbones tomb where  they engage in a serious discussion  about the secret society  i interrupt  do you know if george bush has been here  yet i ask  we could tell you but then we'd have to  kill you joke's one of the officers  we haven't seen him the other one says  but he wouldn't come in the front door  or the side door he'd come in another  entrance  there aren't any other entrances i point  out  yes there are the police officer insists  he gestures to the surrounding building  street hall lc the art gallery  it's all interconnected underneath these  buildings the officer says the tunnels  lead everywhere bush could get in from  practically anywhere on campus  i look up sharply  you've been down there  how do you know that  the police officer says to me i saw the  movie  i pause incredulously to gauge whether  the man is serious  he is  there are only two entrances to this  building i have the floor plans i tell  him  how did you get them the officer gapes  well i could tell you but then i'd have  to kill you i say  the other officer nearly falls off of  his bike  i'm writing a book about skull and bones  i add  good  the officer who saw the movie furrows  his brow and shakes his head i can't  wait for all this crazy [ __ ] to be  exposed he says i hear they have  geronimo's head in there geronimo's  [ __ ] head  can that air on c-span  i thought it was amazing and kind of  funny that even the new haven police  department was pretty weirded out by  skull and bones  but what was even more enticing than  people insisting relentlessly on  skullenbones permitability was that i  found out that at least some of the  members of skull and bones themselves  seem to believe that they possess an  invincible sort of power  a couple of years ago i wrote an article  for the atlantic monthly about george w  bush and skull and bones after that  article came out i received a call at my  office at the new yorker from a man whom  i knew to be a member of skull and bones  and this is an excerpt from the book  about this conversation that we had  he scolded me for writing the article  writing that article was not an ethical  or honorable way to make a decent living  in journalism he condescended  and then asked me how much i had been  paid for the story  when i refused to answer he hung up  15 minutes later he called back  i have just gotten off the phone with  our people  here people i snickered yes our people  he told me that the society demanded to  know where i got my information  i've never been in the tomb and i did  nothing illegal in the process of  reporting this story i replied  well then you must have gotten something  from one of us  tell me whom you spoke to we just want  to talk to them he said  i don't reveal my sources  then he got angry he screamed at me for  a while about how dishonorable i was for  writing that article a lot of people are  very despondent over this he yelled 15  yale juniors are very very upset  i thanked him for telling me his  concerns  there are a lot of us at newspapers and  at political journalism institutions  across the country he coldly hissed good  luck with your career and he slammed  down the phone  another thing that interested me about  skull and bones was whether it has  retained any sort of influence in the  21st century as it did in the 20th  in the early 1900s president supreme  court chief justice and bonesman william  howard taft named at least two of his at  least two bonesmen  to his 11-man cabinet  in the mid-1900s secretary of war henry  stimson surrounded himself with bonesman  whom he then put in charge of the  deployment of the atomic bomb  in the late 1900s george bush as vice  president held skull bones reunions and  as president appointed several skull and  bones members to prestigious positions  and now  in 2002  one of the most fascinating and  surprising things i discovered in the  course of writing this book was that  president george w bush despite his  constant disparagement of yale hasn't  hesitated to turn to skull and bones  himself over and over and over again  here's a passage from secrets of the  tomb about bush and his relationship  with yale  george w bush is unmistakably vocal  about expressing his distance from and  disdain for the elite northeastern  connections that shaped his family's  political dynasty  after graduation in 1968 george w bush  didn't return to yale until 1991 for his  father's honorary degree ceremony and  even then grumbled because he felt the  university had taken way too long to  confer the degree  george w bush's biting dismissals have  been particularly aimed at yale  he has sneered at the intellectual  snobbery of his alma mater a place that  he has said epitomizes a certain east  coast attitude and an intellectual  arrogance he finds unappealing  he has said that he wants to  get away from the snobs that people at  yale felt so intellectually superior and  so righteous  when he finally agreed to speak at yale  in 2001 he used the podium as an  opportunity to make fun of his yale  education  um i don't think that bush is the  purebred texan that he insists he is i  find bush's dichotomy fascinating and i  think his divided persona is sort of  neatly illustrated in where his twin  daughters are at school one of course is  at the university of texas but the other  one is here at yale  so here's a little bit more about the  more hidden yale side of bush  when in 1971 bush was rejected from the  university of texas law school he did  precisely the opposite of what one would  expect from a man who says he has never  relied on his elite northeastern  connections  he called a bonesman  years after other bonesmen had given his  father and his grandfather their first  jobs out of college  robert h gao a bonesman who was in the  same class as bush's cousin ray walker  told the washington post that his  company wasn't looking for anybody at  the time but he hired bush anyway  in 1977 when bush formed his first  company an oil company he called arbusto  energy inc arbusto is spanish for bush  he once again sought the help of skull  and bones relying on his bones uncle  jonathan bush who ran the new york  investment firm j bush and company bush  quickly lined up 565 thousand dollars  from 28 investors one of them put  together 172 550  bonesman william h draper  the wife of federal express ceo  frederick smith a bonesman chaired a one  hundred thousand dollar goal fundraiser  for bush in tennessee  in october of 2000 bonesman stephen  adams who owned adam's outdoor  advertising agency spent one million  dollars on billboard ads for bush  i just contributed because i liked him  adams explained to me  but when i asked adams admitted that he  hasn't ever met bush in person  instead he agreed that he simply had an  affinity for all bonesmen just because  they are members of skull and bones and  as he put it shared that common  experience  loyal bonesman bush returned the favors  in 1984 he jetted to nashville to a  company on a seven city tour gop senate  nominee and current knoxville mayor  victor ashe bones 1967.  while on the tour bush spoke devotedly  about his father to boost ash's gop ties  against an opponent who would later  become much more familiar al gore  also like his father george w bush has  used his presidential power to reward  his fellow bonesmen  one of the first social gatherings  possibly the first that george w bush  had at the white house after his  inauguration was a reunion of his 1968  skull bones class  in november 2001 bush appointed a member  of skull bones as general counsel of the  new federal office of homeland security  and a senior associate counsel to the  president for national security  bonesman frederick smith was reportedly  bush's top choice for secretary of  defense before he had to withdraw from  consideration for health reasons and  bush has also appointed many other  members of skull and bones to  prestigious positions in his  administration including an assistant  attorney general and an adviser to the  secretary of defense  think that george w bush exemplifies the  major paradox of skull and bones which  is that the society on one hand wants to  be secret and invisible but on the other  hand it wants to flaunt its power and  prestige  in his autobiography george w bush  mentions skull and bones only once he  writes  my senior year i joined skull and bones  a secret society so secret i can't say  anything more  it's as if he wants to stamp it on his  resume for us and then gloat that it's  something that we aren't privileged  enough to know anything more about  and finally the aspect about skull and  bones that i find most intriguing is the  relationships between the myths and the  realities of the society  the introduction to skull and bones  which is called the legend of skull and  bones weaves many of the rumors i've  heard and read about the society some of  them are true  some aren't but many of them are merely  exaggerations of reality  for example one of the most scandalous  rumors of skull and bones which some of  you may have heard by now is that  during initiation the neophytes must lie  naked in a coffin and masturbate while  recounting their entire sexual history  to the rest of the members um  this is a rumor that has become so  prevalent that some foreign reporters  i've seen have wondered seriously in  print with some consternation whether  our esteemed presidents bush have  engaged in that honorable activity  as you can read in the chapter on  initiation they probably didn't do that  however sexual history is a crucial part  of skull and bones  during the yale seniors year in skull  and bones there are 15 yale seniors and  they're known within the society as  knights  each night has an evening devoted to him  this probably goes from september to  october and during that evening the  night must stand in front of a painting  of a woman which the society refers to  as connubial bliss they also refer to  this activity as conunubialplus bliss  and before 1991 when women were admitted  they referred to all women everywhere as  connubial bliss anyway they must stand  in front of this painting and they're in  front of the other 14 members they  really will recount their entire sexual  history from the beginning to the end  and  this is supposed to be an activity that  takes anywhere from one to three hours  um i apologize to the yale men who are  there but i think sometimes the length  of the activity  depends on how much they feel like  making up that evening  another myth is that members of skull  and bones must leave the room  immediately if an outsider says either  skull and bones or 322  this rumor caused reporters on the 1988  campaign trail of george bush to wonder  somewhat giddily whether  what would happen if at a press  conference one of them suddenly shouted  skull and bones if george bush would  suddenly hop up from behind the podium  and go dashing out of the room  he wouldn't have not in 1988 but that  was a rule that was in effect until  about the mid-20th century  in secrets of the tomb i also of course  explore some of the more sinister rumors  about the secret society such as uh  whether the bonesmen were in league with  hitler whether they played any part in  choreographing the bay of pigs invasion  and whether they really do have any  relationship to a secret world  government  but in any case um the public's constant  shifting between reality and myth is  really how skull and bones retains its  mystique even now even in 21st century  america  in secrets of the tomb my aim was to  illustrate the truth behind the secret  society and as the book begins  if this truth does not contain all of  the conspiratorial elements that the  skull and bones legend projects it is  perhaps all the more interesting for  that fact  the story of skull and bones is not just  the story of a remarkable secret society  but a remarkable society of secrets some  with basis in truth some nothing but fog  much of the way we understand the world  of power involves myriad assumptions of  